d D4. A lead that large went in a single move."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "G9 stretched Black's G-file wall to the top edge, but it lost 25.9 points and took the score from -1.0 to -26.9. The engine wanted D4, in the open centre-left, where the White stones were still unsettled."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "The 20k answered with F9, which lost 26.3 points and gave nearly all of it back, from -26.9 to -0.6. The engine's choice was C5. The game was level again, and Black had one more chance."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "D3 was the decisive blunder: it lost 32.2 points, taking Black from -1.6 to -33.8, where the engine's choice was D2. White's D2 reply scored -0.1, and the lead hardly moved for the rest of the game. White ended with 4 Black stones captured."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Every script either played a move the player had already chosen (mcp__go__play), parsed returned boards into groups, liberties and regions (the inspect function), replayed the actual move history to verify it, counted final area, or wrote records and notes to files. None of it loops over empty points to score candidates or plays variations forward, and the move notes show the choices were made by hand.", "stop": false}}